# Kazakh → IPA transliteration rules (kk_ipa.rules)
#
# Source-Authors: McCollum, A. G. & Chen, S.
# Source-Year: 2021
# Source-Title: Kazakh
# Source-Container: Journal of the International Phonetic Association 51(2): 276-298
# Source-Id: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100319000185
#
# Simplified: no diphthongs, no dental diacritics, no optional allophones.
#
# Corroborated: Abish, A. (2021) 'Kazakh and Karakalpak', in Johanson &
# Csátó (eds.), The Turkic Languages, 2nd ed., Routledge, 337-353
# (https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003243809-22). Standard Kazakh has ž and
# š; the affricates ǰ and č are "Typical of Chinese Altay variants"
# (p. 338). <у> is the groups uw/üw, finally mostly uː/üː (p. 337) —
# the /uw/ the contextual rules above implement. Her lax near-high set
# is the ɪ ə ʊ ʏ below. Word pairs pinned in
# test_kazakh_routledge_words.py.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# <у> spells two different things
#
# McCollum & Chen give /w/ in the consonant inventory, with уақ /wɑq/
# 'time' and тау /tɑw/ 'mountain', and they also give /uw/ in the
# eleven-vowel inventory /ɑ o ə ʊ æ e ɵ ɪ ʏ ij uw/, with ту /tuw/ 'flag'.
# Both are written <у>, so a single unconditional rule cannot be right:
# writing /w/ everywhere leaves су 'water' as [sw], a syllable with no
# vowel in it.
#
# The letter is the consonant next to a vowel and the vowel elsewhere.
# The vowel is collapsed to its first element under the no-diphthong
# policy this file declares, which is the same treatment /ij/ and /ie/
# already get from И and Е.
#
# The left context is matched against text this file has already
# transliterated, so it lists output vowels; the right context is matched
# against the Cyrillic that has not been converted yet.
$Vsrc = [А а Ә ә Е е Ё ё И и І і О о Ө ө Ұ ұ Ү ү Ы ы Э э Ю ю Я я];
$Vout = [ɑ æ e ə ɪ i o ɵ ʊ ʏ u];

$Vout { У > w ;   $Vout { у > w ;
У } $Vsrc > w ;   у } $Vsrc > w ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One-to-one grapheme → phone map (no diphthongs, no dental marks)
А > ɑ ;  а > ɑ ;
Ә > æ ;  ә > æ ;
Б > b ;  б > b ;
В > v ;  в > v ;
Г > ɡ ;  г > ɡ ;
Ғ > ʁ ;  ғ > ʁ ;
Д > d ;  д > d ;
Е > e ;  е > e ;
Ё > jo ; ё > jo ;
Ж > ʒ ;  ж > ʒ ;
З > z ;  з > z ;
И > i ;  и > i ;
Й > j ;  й > j ;
К > k ;  к > k ;
Қ > q ;  қ > q ;
Л > l ;  л > l ;
М > m ;  м > m ;
Н > n ;  н > n ;
Ң > ŋ ;  ң > ŋ ;
О > o ;  о > o ;
Ө > ɵ ;  ө > ɵ ;
П > p ;  п > p ;
Р > r ;  р > r ;
С > s ;  с > s ;
Т > t ;  т > t ;
У > u ;  у > u ;   # the vowel /uw/, collapsed; /w/ is handled above
Ұ > ʊ ;  ұ > ʊ ;
Ү > ʏ ;  ү > ʏ ;
Ф > f ;  ф > f ;
Х > χ ;  х > χ ;
Һ > h ;  һ > h ;
Ц > t͡s ; ц > t͡s ;
Ч > t͡ʃ ;  ч > t͡ʃ ;
Ш > ʃ ;  ш > ʃ ;
Щ > ɕː ;  щ > ɕː ;
Ъ > ʔ ;  ъ > ʔ ;
Ы > ə ;  ы > ə ;
І > ɪ ;  і > ɪ ;
Ь > ʲ ;  ь > ʲ ;
Э > e ;  э > e ;
Ю > ju ; ю > ju ;
Я > ja ; я > ja ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Letters outside the Kazakh alphabet that reach the corpus in quoted or
# mixed-script material. Without a rule they passed through as Cyrillic
# into what is supposed to be IPA. Each takes the value our own sourced
# rule file gives it for the language that owns it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ѳ > θ ;  ѳ > θ ;    # FITA, Greek theta loans; ky_ipa.rules
Ҳ > h ;  ҳ > h ;    # Uzbek; uzc_ipa.rules (Ido 2025)
Ҥ > ŋ ;  ҥ > ŋ ;    # EN WITH TAIL, velar nasal; ky_ipa.rules
# U+045E is SHORT U, Belarusian /w/. Uzbek repurposes the letter for /o/
# and uzc_ipa.rules writes it that way; outside Uzbek it keeps its own
# value, as ky_ipa.rules also has it.
Ў > w ;  ў > w ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional phonetic refinements — keep commented to omit allophones
# ɡ > ɣ  || _[ɑ ʊ ə o ʏ] ;
# k > q  || _[ɑ ʊ ə o ʏ] ;
# v > β̞ || $V _ $V ;
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:: NFC ;
